> Difference between **CommonJS (require)** vs **ES Modules (import/export)**

---

# 1️⃣ Syntax Difference

| CommonJS (Old Node)     | ES Modules (Modern JS)                 |
| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| `require()`             | `import`                               |
| `module.exports`        | `export`                               |
| Runs by default in Node | Need `"type":"module"` in package.json |
| Synchronous loading     | Asynchronous loading                   |
| Mostly backend          | Frontend + Backend                     |

---

# 2️⃣ Example with Real-Time Use Case

---

## 🧾 Case: Payment Service

---

## CommonJS (require / module.exports)

👉 Used in many existing Node.js projects

### payment.js

```js
function pay(amount) {
  console.log(`Paid ${amount}`);
}

module.exports = pay;
```

### app.js

```js
const pay = require("./payment");

pay(500);
```

### Where used in real life

* Old Express projects
* Legacy backend systems
* Many production Node APIs today

---

## ES Modules (import / export)

👉 Modern way (React + Node + Next.js)

### payment.js

```js
export function pay(amount) {
  console.log(`Paid ${amount}`);
}
```

### app.js

```js
import { pay } from "./payment.js";

pay(500);
```

### Where used in real life

* React apps
* Next.js
* Modern Node microservices
* TypeScript projects

---

# 3️⃣ Default Export vs Named Export

### Default Export (One main thing)

```js
// tax.js
export default function tax(price) {
  return price * 0.18;
}

// app.js
import tax from "./tax.js";
```

👉 Real Use: Main service (Payment gateway, DB connection)

---

### Named Export (Multiple utilities)

```js
// auth.js
export function login() {}
export function logout() {}
export function verifyToken() {}

// app.js
import { login, logout } from "./auth.js";
```

👉 Real Use: Auth module

---

# 4️⃣ Key Concept Difference (Important)

| Feature         | CommonJS        | ES Modules          |
| --------------- | --------------- | ------------------- |
| Loading         | Blocking        | Non-blocking        |
| Performance     | Slower startup  | Better optimization |
| Tree shaking    | ❌ No            | ✅ Yes               |
| Used by         | Node older apps | Modern JS ecosystem |
| Top-level await | ❌               | ✅                   |

---

# 🧠 Simple Memory Trick

> **require = old Node way**
> **import = modern JavaScript way**

---

# 🎯 20-Second Interview Answer

You can say:

> CommonJS uses require and module.exports and loads modules synchronously, mainly used in older Node.js applications.
> ES Modules use import/export and load modules asynchronously, support tree-shaking and are used in modern frameworks like React and Next.js.
> Nowadays most new projects prefer ES Modules.

